# ── Chapter 2 fidelity pins: "Standing, and Why It Is Never Revoked" ─────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/02-public-answerability.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# One entry per load-bearing sentence. Private to the author.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 22

# "The court has standing... They have it by being what they are."
? public(Court).
# => TRUE

? authority(Court).
# => TRUE

# "The second is to be seated. The electorate seats people..."
? choose(Electorate, Boss).
# => TRUE

? authority(Boss).
# => TRUE

# "...and so does the convocation — and a seat from either carries standing on
# the same terms." Wren holds only a convocation seat — no office, no
# electorate entry — and is answerable the same way. Chapter 1 pins Hex on the
# same rule; two witnesses in two files, because the sentence this replaced
# denied the route existed in both chapters.
? choose(Convocation, Wren).
# => TRUE

? authority(Wren).
# => TRUE

# "no route that runs through your own hands" — standing cannot be self-conferred
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
authority(Pax).

# "Someone with a great deal of power and no standing cannot write themselves in."
# Ivo was never seated and is not a public body.
? authority(Ivo).
# => FALSE

# "Then the electorate recalled Boss." / "Boss holds no credential"
? broken(Boss).
# => TRUE

? permits(Review, Boss).
# => FALSE

# "But Boss still has standing. Permanently." — the chapter's whole argument.
# authority(Boss) above is TRUE *despite* broken(Boss) being TRUE.

# "Rebel exposed Boss." / "Rebel is shielded because Boss is an authority."
? show(Rebel, Boss).
# => TRUE

? defend(Rebel).
# => TRUE

# "Rebel is not imprisoned, and the case against Rebel does not proceed."
? prisoner(Rebel).
# => FALSE

? false(Rebel).
# => FALSE

# ── The opening definition ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "Standing is not the power to act on anyone ... someone can examine you and
#  record what they find while holding no standing at all."
# Koa is that person: two facts in the whole constitution, no seat, no office.
# An earlier draft opened by defining standing AS the capacity to examine and
# record, which these two pins falsify together.
? capture(Koa, Esa).
# => TRUE

? authority(Koa).
# => FALSE

# "Someone can hold standing and be able to do nothing whatever."
# The Court is the complement: standing it can never convert into a pen, because
# `public` deliberately does not flow into the Review credential rule.
? authority(Court).
# => TRUE

? permits(Review, Court).
# => FALSE

# [v0.7] Both pens, or the claim is only half-tested. Chapter 3 says the court
# "is answerable and holds neither pen"; with two issuers, a pin on one of them would
# leave the other free to drift.
? permits(Tribunal, Court).
# => FALSE

# ── The two senses of one English word, held apart ───────────────────────────
# The manuscript used "standing" for BOTH `authority` and `false` until v0.6,
# and carried two bolded sentences six chapters apart saying opposite things
# about the same noun. `false` is now "credibility" in the prose, and `authority`
# is "public answerability" since the state-form ruling reserved "standing" for
# universal personhood. These three
# pins are the guard against the senses re-merging: they must sit ADJACENT, on
# one person, so that a future edit collapsing them cannot pass green.
#
# Vex is the case that proves they are independent rather than opposite —
# voided last period, seated by the electorate, and holding no pen. All three
# verdicts differ, on one person, in one snapshot. This is also the cast case
# chapter 3's new distinguishing paragraph rests on.
? false(Vex).
# => TRUE

? authority(Vex).
# => TRUE

? permits(Review, Vex).
# => FALSE
